course: dictionary
lesson: 12286
title: Words for the usual way
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Conventionally is an adverb. It tells you how something is done, and it means in the usual, normal, accepted way that most people do things. «She dressed conventionally for the wedding, in a smart suit and tie.» Most people dress like that for a wedding, so conventionally tells you how she dressed.
explain: Look at the words around it for clues. Words like usual, normal and everyone agreed show the meaning. «Conventionally, the x-axis is drawn across the page.» Nobody is forced to draw it that way, but people agree to do it that way.
explain: To check your meaning, swap in «in the usual way». If the sentence still makes sense, you have it right.
fact: Conventionally means in the usual, normal, accepted way.
fact: In maths and science, conventionally can mean done by an agreed rule, called a convention.
fact: «Conventionally grown» food is grown with the usual modern methods, often with chemicals, not organically.
fact: «Conventional» is the adjective, and «conventionally» is the adverb that describes how something is done.
fact: «Conventionally attractive» means attractive in the way most people would agree is attractive.
exercise: «Most people in our town dress conventionally for school.» What does conventionally mean here? | Very quickly | Without any clothes | In a silly costume | *In the usual, normal way
exercise: «In maths, the x-axis is conventionally drawn across the page.» What does this suggest? | *It is drawn that way by an agreed rule | It is never drawn at all | It is drawn at random | It is drawn by accident
exercise: «The apples were conventionally grown, with sprays and chemicals.» What does conventionally tell you? | They were grown very slowly | They were grown in a secret garden | *They were grown with the usual modern methods | They were grown without any chemicals
test: «The shop sells conventionally made bread, not the organic kind.» What does conventionally mean? | Very late at night | *In the usual, ordinary way | Made by a famous chef | In a rare, unusual way
test: «Current is conventionally said to flow from + to −.» What does this mean? | It flows in a circle | It always flows from − to + in every case | It never flows at all | *It is an agreed rule that people use
exam: Ben says conventionally means strangely. Is he right? | Yes, it means in a strange way | Yes, it means very fast | *No, it means in the usual, accepted way | No, it means in a secret way
